.\" deviceterm(1) manual page.
.\" Groups and orders its COMMANDS to match the topic table in
.\" Sources/DeviceTermCLI/HelpCatalog.swift, which is what
.\" `deviceterm help` renders. Update both together when adding or
.\" renaming a verb; HelpCatalogTests guards the verb set, not the
.\" prose, so the wording here is maintained by hand.
.TH DEVICETERM 1 "2026-08-03" "deviceterm" "DeviceTerm Manual"
.SH NAME
deviceterm \- macOS terminal where Apple devices behave as first-class panes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B deviceterm
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.RB [ --pane
.IR ref ]
.RB [ --json ]
.PP
.B deviceterm help
.RI [ command | topic ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B deviceterm
is a macOS-native terminal application in which Apple-platform devices —
booted simulators (iOS, watchOS, iPadOS, tvOS) and physically-connected
iPhones/iPads — attach as first-class panes inside the tab. The
.B deviceterm
CLI runs inside a tab and drives the tab's device panes: touch,
hardware buttons, keyboard, rotation, the watchOS Digital Crown, and
accessibility inspection. Pass
.B --pane
.I ref
to disambiguate when the tab shows more than one device pane (resolving a
shortId, name, sim UDID, physical deviceId, or paneId).
.SH COMMANDS
Grouped below in the order
.B deviceterm help
lists them. Run
.B deviceterm help
.I command
to read that command's reference page, or
.B deviceterm agents
for the workflow and triage guide.
.SS Drive the device
Coords are normalized to what the device is showing; (0,0) top-left,
(1,1) bottom-right. A Simulator's observed rotation is followed; a
physical device is assumed portrait until deviceterm rotates it.
.TP
.B tap " " \fIx\fR " " \fIy\fR
Single discrete tap.
.TP
.B swipe " " \fIfromX\fR " " \fIfromY\fR " " \fItoX\fR " " \fItoY\fR " " [\fB--duration\fR \fIms\fR] " " [\fB--hold\fR \fIms\fR]
Interpolated drag at ~60 Hz. Default 200 ms.
.B --hold
adds an active dwell at the end point (the finger is reported,
decelerated to a stop, before lifting).
.TP
.B long-press " " \fIx\fR " " \fIy\fR " " [\fB--duration\fR \fIms\fR]
Down at point, hold, up. Default 500 ms.
.TP
.B pinch " " \fIf1x f1y f2x f2y tf1x tf1y tf2x tf2y\fR " " [\fB--duration\fR \fIms\fR]
Two-finger interpolated path. Default 300 ms.
.TP
.B app-switcher
Open the iOS App Switcher with an edge-tagged swipe up from the bottom
edge with a dwell, a system gesture rather than a content swipe.
Confirmed in portrait and both landscape orientations; upside-down has no
edge value that arms the recognizer. A physical device that doesn't
support the edge gesture falls back to a consumer-HID Home double-press.
.SS Hardware input
.TP
.B button " " \fIhome\fR|\fIlock\fR|\fIside\fR|\fIapple-pay\fR|\fIsiri\fR|\fIdigital-crown\fR
Press a hardware button. Names are accepted in kebab-case (canonical),
snake_case, camelCase, or all lowercase. Use
.B crown
for watchOS rotation.
.TP
.B key " " \fIkeyCode\fR " " \fIdown\fR|\fIup\fR
kVK virtual key code (decimal or 0x-hex). Discrete events.
.TP
.B text " " \fIstring\fR
Type an ASCII string.
.TP
.B rotate " " \fIportrait\fR|\fIportrait-upside-down\fR|\fIlandscape-left\fR|\fIlandscape-right\fR|\fIleft\fR|\fIright\fR
Set device orientation. Orientation names match UIKit's UIDeviceOrientation,
in the same spellings
.B button
accepts.
.I left
and
.I right
turn the device 90 degrees from wherever DeviceTerm last put it. It tracks only
the rotations it performed, so the first relative rotate after attaching to an
already-turned device steps from the wrong place.
.TP
.B crown " " \fIdelta\fR " " [\fB--velocity\fR \fIv\fR] " " [\fB--duration\fR \fIms\fR]
Rotate the watchOS Digital Crown. Signed
.IR delta ;
positive is forward/down.
.B --velocity
is accepted on the wire but ignored at the daemon.
.SS Inspect
.TP
.B ax tree
Dump the frontmost iOS app's accessibility tree as JSON. On watchOS often
returns an empty walk by design; use
.B ax sweep
as the documented workaround.
.TP
.B ax point " " \fIx\fR " " \fIy\fR
Single AX element at a normalized point, in the same displayed space the
coordinate-bearing input verbs take. Node frames are in that space but are
not normalized: divide the centre's x and y by the root frame's width and
height before querying it.
.TP
.B ax sweep " " [\fB--step\fR \fI0..1\fR] " " [\fB--budget\fR \fIms\fR]
Grid-walk the screen via objectAtPoint and aggregate unique elements.
Default step 0.05, clamped silently into [0.02, 0.5]; the result echoes the
step used. A completed sweep makes ceil(1/step)^2 point queries, and a
control narrower than the step can fall between samples and read as absent.
.B --budget
bounds how long the daemon may spend scheduling those queries: default
10000, clamped into [0, 60000], echoed as budgetMs in the result. The 0.02
floor makes 2500 queries and normally exhausts the default, so raise it when
the result comes back truncated.
If the daemon finds its budget spent before the grid is done, it stops
before the next query, sets
.B truncated
in the result, and adds a
.B note
saying what to try: a larger --budget, or a coarser --step when the budget
was already at the ceiling.
.SS Devices
.TP
.B devices list
The aggregate live roster: owned booted sims + connected physical devices,
each annotated with its attach/ownership state. Physical devices also
report model and OS version, disambiguating two devices that share a
name.
.TP
.B device attach " " \fIref\fR
The unified explicit-attach verb. Resolve
.I ref
against
.B devices list
(sim UDID, physical deviceId, or name) to claim a booted/orphan sim or
mirror a connected physical device into the caller's tab. A bare UDID
absent from the roster is claimed as an externally-booted sim.
This is the only explicit-attach verb; there is no
.BR "pane attach" .
.TP
.B panes list
List the calling tab's device panes.
.SS Manage the workspace
Back-channel verbs.
.TP
.B tab open " " [\fB--window\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fB--cwd\fR \fIpath\fR] " " [\fB--cmd\fR \fIcmd\fR]
Requires a live automation grant. Mint a fresh agent-role tab in the
chosen window (default: the window holding the calling tab).
.B --cwd
sets the shell's startup directory; without it the new tab inherits the
GUI login-shell default.
.B --cmd
is typed into the new shell after attach, so the command runs once and
the user is left at an interactive prompt.
.TP
.B tab close " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fB--mode\fR \fIdetach\fR|\fIshutdown\fR]
Close the named tab. Default
.B --tab
is the caller's current tab. Without a live automation grant, reaches only
your own tab, and only while it holds that tab's single terminal.
.B --mode
controls what happens to any linked sims.
.TP
.B tab rename " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fIname\fR]
Apply a manual title; omit
.I name
to restore the automatic title. Without a live automation grant, reaches
only a tab you own a terminal in.
.TP
.B tab select " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR]
Requires a live automation grant, including for the caller's own tab.
Focus the named tab in its window.
.TP
.B tab info " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR]
Print a structured description of the named tab (role, session, linked sim
panes).
.B --json
emits the raw payload.
.TP
.B tab move " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fB--to\fR \fIindex\fR] " " [\fB--to-window\fR \fIref\fR]
Requires a live automation grant, including for the caller's own tab.
Reorder the named tab within its window
.RB ( --to )
or move it to another window
.RB ( --to-window ,
optionally at
.BR --to ;
default appends at the end). At least one of
.B --to
/
.B --to-window
is required. Mirrors dragging a tab in the strip.
.TP
.B tab set-protected " " \fItrue\fR|\fIfalse\fR " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR]
Hide the named tab from other sessions, or unhide it. A protected tab and
its panes drop out of every other session's
.B tabs list
and
.B windows list
views, can't be reached by their refs, and are refused to
.B tab send-input
/
.B tab capture
even from an automation tab. The caller's own views are unchanged, and
only a tab the caller owns a terminal in can be flipped. The value takes
true/false, yes/no, on/off, or 1/0.
.TP
.B tab send-input " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fB--type-delay\fR \fIms\fR] " " \fItext\fR
Requires a live automation grant. Authority is the grant, not a
role. Write
.I text
into the resolved tab's terminal as though the user had typed it. Control
sequences (\\n, \\r, \\x03) flow through libghostty's input pipeline like
real keypresses. A caller whose session holds no grant is rejected at the
dispatcher's scope check with
.BR error.scope_violation .
The receipt reports only the byte count, never the typed text.
.B --type-delay
.I ms
animates the injection one character at a time (for screencasts); omit
it for the instant one-shot. The verb returns as soon as the typing is
enqueued (it does not block for the animation), and concurrent paced
calls to one tab type out in order. Pacing is per-character, so keep
paced text to plain commands plus a trailing newline. The delay is
capped at 1000 ms.
.TP
.B tab capture " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR]
Requires a live automation grant. Print the resolved tab's
currently-visible viewport (the rendered terminal screen) to stdout.
Captures the visible viewport only; there are no scrollback or
line-count flags. Human mode emits the raw text;
.B --json
emits a
.B {text}
object. A caller whose session holds no grant is rejected with
.BR error.scope_violation .
.TP
.B tabs list
List every daemon session you can see. A protected session is listed
only for its owner.
.TP
.B tabs current
Print the caller's own tab row.
.TP
.B pane open --terminal " " [\fB--tab\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fB--cwd\fR \fIpath\fR] " " [\fB--cmd\fR \fIcmd\fR]
Open a fresh terminal pane alongside the tab's existing panes, splitting
the tab rather than opening a new one. Naming another tab with
.B --tab
requires a live automation grant.
.B --cwd
and
.B --cmd
carry the same meaning as on
.BR "tab open" .
.TP
.B pane close " " [\fB--pane\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fB--mode\fR \fIdetach\fR|\fIshutdown\fR]
Detach or shut down the named sim pane. Without a live automation grant,
reaches only panes in a tab you own a terminal in.
.TP
.B pane info " " [\fB--pane\fR \fIref\fR]
Print a structured description of the named sim pane.
.TP
.B pane rename " " [\fB--pane\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fIname\fR]
Not implemented; returns
.B intent.internalError
.TP
.B pane move " " [\fB--pane\fR \fIref\fR] " " \fB--to-tab\fR \fIref\fR
Not implemented; returns
.B intent.internalError
.TP
.B window open
Requires a live automation grant. Mint a new window with one fresh
agent-role tab.
.TP
.B window close " " [\fB--window\fR \fIref\fR] " " [\fB--mode\fR \fIdetach\fR|\fIshutdown\fR]
Close the named window. Refuses a window holding a tab you can't see, or
one holding a tab you don't solely own, unless you hold a live automation
grant.
.TP
.B window focus " " [\fB--window\fR \fIref\fR]
Requires a live automation grant, including for the caller's own window.
Bring the named window forward, activating DeviceTerm if another app is
in front.
.TP
.B windows list " " [\fB--all\fR]
List the visible windows.
.B --json
emits a
.B WindowInfoPayload
array.
.TP
.B with-pane " " \fIref\fR " " \fIcmd\fR " " [\fIargs\fR ...]
Resolve the device pane matching
.I ref
(shortId, name, sim UDID, physical deviceId, or paneId prefix), inject
.B DEVICETERM_TARGET_PANE
into the env, and exec
.IR cmd .
Downstream
.B deviceterm tap
/
.B swipe
/ etc. inside
.I cmd
auto-target without
.BR --pane .
.PP
Refs accept:
.B --tab
takes a session UUID, a shortId, a quoted name, or the literal
.BR current ;
.B --pane
takes a paneId UUID, a shortId, or
.BR current ;
.B --window
takes a 1-based index or
.BR current .
.SS Setup and health
.TP
.B doctor
Check the pieces every other verb depends on: the session env vars,
whether
.B xcrun
resolves to the shim, the daemon socket and ping, and whether this
session is live and authenticates. Also reports the caller's identity,
its linked device panes, and the methods its transport and grant admit.
.B --json
emits the same report as an object. Exits 1 when any axis fails;
warnings alone still exit 0.
.TP
.B events
Subscribe to this session's event stream as one JSON object per line.
Long-running; exits on daemon EOF or Ctrl-C.
.TP
.B version
Print versions of deviceterm + daemon + RPC wire + macOS.
.TP
.B dump-config
Print every recognized
.I ~/.config/deviceterm/config
key with its current value and source layer.
.TP
.B completions install " " \fIzsh\fR|\fIbash\fR|\fIfish\fR
Generate and install the per-shell completion script. Prints the install
path and a one-line activation hint.
.SS Docs
.TP
.B help " " [\fIcommand\fR|\fItopic\fR]
Print the command list, or one command's reference page.
.B --help
and
.B -h
are the same verb. The concepts named at the foot of the list
(targeting, refs, output, troubleshooting) are addressable the same way.
.TP
.B agents
Print the workflow and triage guide: how a device pane attaches to a
tab, recipes for the common automation shapes, and the permission model
behind the errors the CLI returns.
.SH OUTPUT
Data commands (lists, receipts) support
.B --json
for machine-readable output: lists become JSON arrays, receipts become
JSON objects with the same fields as the human form. Successful output
goes to stdout; errors stay on stderr in human form. Documentation
commands
.RB ( --help ,
.BR agents )
are prose either way; AX commands always emit JSON.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_SESSION
UUID identifying the caller's terminal session. Daemon-minted, injected by
the GUI when the terminal opens; each terminal split has its own session.
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_SESSION_CAP
Capability token for the session, validated on every session-scoped RPC.
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_DAEMON_SOCK
Path to the daemon's Unix-domain socket. Defaults to the per-user
location under Application Support.
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_SHIM_DIR
Per-session
.I bin/
holding the xcrun/simctl shim and the deviceterm-cli symlink.
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_TARGET_PANE
Optional device-pane target consumed by pane-targeted commands. Set by
.B deviceterm with-pane
(to the resolved pane's key) so child processes auto-target.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/deviceterm/config
User overrides. See
.B deviceterm dump-config
for recognized keys.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Caches/deviceterm/sessions/<sessionId>/
Per-session scratch directory: shim
.IR bin/ ,
the zsh
.IR ZDOTDIR ,
and the owner manifest.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Pane attachment is a precondition for input and AX commands. If
.B deviceterm tap
returns
.RI \(lq no device pane in this session\(rq ,
boot a sim from this terminal with
.B xcrun simctl boot \fIUDID\fR
\(em the deviceterm shim intercepts the boot and creates the pane. Custom
helpers that bypass
.B xcrun simctl boot
won't create a pane. Run
.B deviceterm doctor
for an env + daemon + session health check, or
.B deviceterm agents
for the full triage guide.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR simctl (1),
.BR xcrun (1)
.PP
.B deviceterm help
\(em the command list, one line per verb.
.PP
.B deviceterm help \fIcommand\fR
\(em that command's reference page.
.PP
.B deviceterm agents
\(em workflow and triage guide.
